<h1>SDR Driver Abstraction</h1>
<p class="tagline">How readsb talks to eight different radio hardware types through a single interface — and the C pattern that makes it work.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="1">
    <span>1. Why This Matters for readsb</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>readsb can receive ADS-B signals from many different SDR (Software Defined Radio) devices: the cheap RTL-SDR USB dongle, a BladeRF, a HackRF, a PlutoSDR, or even a Beast receiver. Each device has its own library and API.</p>

<p>Without an abstraction layer, the main program would be full of <code>if (using_rtlsdr) { ... } else if (using_bladerf) { ... }</code> branches — fragile, hard to extend, and mixed with business logic. The driver abstraction layer isolates all that hardware-specific code so the rest of the program just calls <code>sdrOpen()</code>, <code>sdrRun()</code>, and <code>sdrClose()</code> and never has to think about which device is attached.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Real files:</strong> <code>sdr.h</code> and <code>sdr.c</code> define the public interface and dispatch table. Each device lives in its own file pair: <code>sdr_rtlsdr.c</code>, <code>sdr_bladerf.c</code>, <code>sdr_hackrf.c</code>, and so on.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="2">
    <span>2. The Core Concept: Function Pointers in C</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>In C++ you get virtual functions. In C you build the same mechanism by hand using <em>function pointers</em>. A function pointer is a variable that holds the address of a function. You can call it just like a normal function.</p>

<pre><span class="kw">int</span> <span class="fn">add</span>(<span class="kw">int</span> a, <span class="kw">int</span> b) { <span class="kw">return</span> a + b; }
<span class="kw">int</span> <span class="fn">mul</span>(<span class="kw">int</span> a, <span class="kw">int</span> b) { <span class="kw">return</span> a * b; }

<span class="cm">// Declare a pointer to a function that takes two ints and returns int</span>
<span class="kw">int</span> (*<span class="fn">op</span>)(<span class="kw">int</span>, <span class="kw">int</span>);

op = add;           <span class="cm">// point at add()</span>
op(<span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>);           <span class="cm">// calls add(3, 4) → 7</span>

op = mul;           <span class="cm">// now point at mul()</span>
op(<span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>);           <span class="cm">// calls mul(3, 4) → 12</span></pre>

<p>Put several function pointers inside a struct and you have a <em>vtable</em> — the C version of a class with virtual methods.</p>

<pre><span class="kw">typedef struct</span> {
    <span class="kw">void</span> (*<span class="fn">open</span>)();
    <span class="kw">void</span> (*<span class="fn">run</span>)();
    <span class="kw">void</span> (*<span class="fn">close</span>)();
} <span class="ty">Device</span>;

<span class="kw">void</span> <span class="fn">rtl_open</span>()  { <span class="cm">/* open an RTL-SDR dongle */</span> }
<span class="kw">void</span> <span class="fn">rtl_run</span>()   { <span class="cm">/* start async reads */</span>       }
<span class="kw">void</span> <span class="fn">rtl_close</span>() { <span class="cm">/* clean up */</span>               }

<span class="ty">Device</span> <span class="fn">rtlsdr</span> = { rtl_open, rtl_run, rtl_close };

<span class="cm">// Anywhere in the program:</span>
rtlsdr.open();    <span class="cm">// dispatches to rtl_open()</span>
rtlsdr.run();     <span class="cm">// dispatches to rtl_run()</span></pre>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="3">
    <span>3. In This Project — sdr_handler and the Dispatch Table</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>readsb uses exactly this pattern. Here is the actual struct from <span class="filepath">sdr.c:49</span>:</p>

<pre><span class="kw">typedef struct</span> {
    <span class="kw">void</span>  (*<span class="fn">initConfig</span>)();
    <span class="kw">bool</span>  (*<span class="fn">handleOption</span>)(<span class="kw">int</span>, <span class="kw">char</span>*);
    <span class="kw">bool</span>  (*<span class="fn">open</span>)();
    <span class="kw">void</span>  (*<span class="fn">run</span>)();
    <span class="kw">void</span>  (*<span class="fn">cancel</span>)();
    <span class="kw">void</span>  (*<span class="fn">close</span>)();
    <span class="kw">const char</span> *name;        <span class="cm">// "rtlsdr", "hackrf", etc.</span>
    <span class="ty">sdr_type_t</span> sdr_type;    <span class="cm">// enum value</span>
    <span class="ty">pthread_t</span>  manageThread;
    <span class="kw">void</span>  (*<span class="fn">setGain</span>)(<span class="kw">char</span> *reason);
} <span class="ty">sdr_handler</span>;</pre>

<p>Then all the drivers are registered in one static array — the <strong>dispatch table</strong> — at <span class="filepath">sdr.c:94</span>:</p>

<pre><span class="kw">static</span> <span class="ty">sdr_handler</span> sdr_handlers[] = {

<span class="pp">#ifdef ENABLE_RTLSDR</span>
    { rtlsdrInitConfig, rtlsdrHandleOption, rtlsdrOpen,
      rtlsdrRun, rtlsdrCancel, rtlsdrClose,
      <span class="str">"rtlsdr"</span>, SDR_RTLSDR, <span class="num">0</span>, rtlsdrSetGain },
<span class="pp">#endif</span>

<span class="pp">#ifdef ENABLE_HACKRF</span>
    { hackRFInitConfig, hackRFHandleOption, hackRFOpen,
      hackRFRun, noCancel, hackRFClose,
      <span class="str">"hackrf"</span>, SDR_HACKRF, <span class="num">0</span>, noSetGain },
<span class="pp">#endif</span>

    <span class="cm">/* ... more drivers ... */</span>

    { noInitConfig, noHandleOption, noOpen, noRun, noCancel, noClose,
      <span class="str">"none"</span>, SDR_NONE, <span class="num">0</span>, noSetGain },

    { NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, SDR_NONE, <span class="num">0</span>, NULL }
    <span class="cm">/* sentinel: marks end of array */</span>
};</pre>

<div class="callout tip">
  <strong>The sentinel pattern:</strong> The last entry has all <code>NULL</code> pointers. Loops can iterate until they find a NULL name instead of needing a separate length variable. You'll see this throughout C code that deals with lists of things.
</div>

<h3>How the right driver is selected at runtime</h3>
<p><span class="filepath">sdr.c:160</span> — <code>current_handler()</code> just walks the array comparing <code>sdr_type</code>:</p>

<pre><span class="kw">static</span> <span class="ty">sdr_handler</span> *<span class="fn">current_handler</span>() {
    <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">int</span> i = <span class="num">0</span>; sdr_handlers[i].name; ++i) {
        <span class="kw">if</span> (Modes.sdr_type == sdr_handlers[i].sdr_type) {
            <span class="kw">return</span> &amp;sdr_handlers[i];
        }
    }
    <span class="kw">return</span> &amp;unsupported_handler;  <span class="cm">// graceful fallback</span>
}

<span class="cm">// Then sdrOpen() is simply:</span>
<span class="kw">bool</span> <span class="fn">sdrOpen</span>() {
    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">current_handler</span>()-&gt;open();  <span class="cm">// calls rtlsdrOpen, hackRFOpen, etc.</span>
}</pre>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Key insight:</strong> The entire rest of the program calls <code>sdrOpen()</code>. It never calls <code>rtlsdrOpen()</code> directly. The dispatch table is the only place that knows which function to call.
</div>

<h3>Architecture at a glance</h3>
<div class="diagram">  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                readsb.c (main)                  │
  │         sdrOpen() / sdrRun() / sdrClose()       │
  └──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘
                     │ calls
  ┌──────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐
  │                  sdr.c                          │
  │  current_handler() → looks up sdr_handlers[]   │
  │       dispatches to the right function ptr      │
  └──┬───────┬──────┬──────┬───────┬───────┬───────┘
     │       │      │      │       │       │
  rtlsdr  bladerf hackrf plutosdr beast  ifile
  .c/.h   .c/.h  .c/.h  .c/.h  .c/.h  .c/.h</div>

