<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="4">
    <span>4. The Null-Object Pattern — no* Functions</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>Not every driver supports every operation. The Beast receiver has no <code>run()</code> loop (it uses a different data path), and most drivers don't implement <code>cancel()</code>. Rather than putting <code>NULL</code> function pointers in the table (which would crash on dereference), readsb uses <em>no-op stub functions</em> — <span class="filepath">sdr.c:62</span>:</p>

<pre><span class="kw">static void</span> <span class="fn">noRun</span>()   { }          <span class="cm">// do nothing, safely</span>
<span class="kw">static void</span> <span class="fn">noCancel</span>() { }
<span class="kw">static void</span> <span class="fn">noClose</span>() { }
<span class="kw">static void</span> <span class="fn">noSetGain</span>() { }</pre>

<p>In the dispatch table, Beast uses <code>noRun</code> and <code>noCancel</code> because it has no async read loop:</p>

<pre>{ beastInitConfig, beastHandleOption, beastOpen,
  <span class="fn">noRun</span>, <span class="fn">noCancel</span>, <span class="fn">noClose</span>,
  <span class="str">"modesbeast"</span>, SDR_MODESBEAST, <span class="num">0</span>, noSetGain },</pre>

<p>This is the <strong>Null Object pattern</strong>: instead of checking for <code>NULL</code> before every call, you provide an object that implements the interface by doing nothing. The caller never needs an <code>if</code> guard.</p>

<div class="callout warning">
  <strong>Compare:</strong> There is also <code>noOpen()</code> (prints "No SDR device selected") and <code>unsupportedOpen()</code> (prints "Support for this SDR type was not enabled in this build"). These are <em>not</em> silent — they report meaningful error messages. Not all no-ops are silent; some carry intent.
</div>

