<div class="quiz-block">

  <!-- Q1 -->
  <div class="question" data-question="1">
    <p>Q1. What type is stored in the <code>open</code> field of <code>sdr_handler</code>?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="A void pointer can hold any address but has no type information about what arguments to pass, so you can't call it safely. The field uses a typed function pointer.">void* (generic pointer)</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="This is the return type of open(), not the type of the field itself. The field is a pointer to a function that returns bool.">bool (return value)</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. bool (*open)() is a pointer to a function that takes no arguments and returns bool. The -> operator on the struct dereferences this to call it.">A pointer to a function returning bool</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="sdr_type_t is the enum for identifying which driver is active. It's a separate field, not the function pointer.">sdr_type_t (enum)</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <!-- Q2 -->
  <div class="question" data-question="2">
    <p>Q2. If HackRF support is not compiled in (<code>ENABLE_HACKRF</code> not defined), what happens when the user passes <code>--device-type hackrf</code>?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="current_handler() returns unsupported_handler when no matching sdr_type is found — it doesn't crash, it falls back gracefully.">The program crashes with a null pointer dereference</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="HackRF is not in the dispatch table at all when ENABLE_HACKRF is undefined. sdrHandleOption won't match it.">HackRF's initConfig runs but open() does nothing</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Exactly right. When ENABLE_HACKRF is not defined, the hackrf row is absent from sdr_handlers[]. sdrHandleOption() prints the list of recognised types and returns false. Later, current_handler() falls back to unsupported_handler, which prints 'Support for this SDR type was not enabled in this build.'">sdrHandleOption prints the list of supported types and fails; later calls fall back to unsupported_handler</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="There's no default fallback to rtlsdr. Each handler is distinct and selected explicitly.">It silently falls back to the rtlsdr driver</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <!-- Q3 -->
  <div class="question" data-question="3">
    <p>Q3. The Beast receiver entry uses <code>noRun</code> in the dispatch table. What does <code>noRun()</code> do?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="noRun is a real function — it just has an empty body. It's not NULL, so calling it is safe.">It is NULL; calling it would segfault</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. noRun is defined as 'static void noRun() {}' — an empty function. The caller can safely call it without any if-guard. This is the Null Object pattern.">It has an empty body and returns immediately, doing nothing</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="noRun doesn't print anything. noOpen() does ('No SDR device or file selected'), but noRun is truly silent.">It prints a warning to stderr</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="noRun does not call rtlsdrRun or any other run function. It's truly a no-op.">It delegates to the rtlsdr run function as a fallback</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <!-- Q4 -->
  <div class="question" data-question="4">
    <p>Q4. Why does the <code>sdr_handlers[]</code> array end with a <code>{ NULL, NULL, …, NULL }</code> entry?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="C doesn't require array terminators. You could store the count separately — but a sentinel is often cleaner.">C arrays require a terminator or they overflow</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. Both sdrInitConfig() and current_handler() loop with 'while (sdr_handlers[i].name)'. When name is NULL the loop stops. This is called the sentinel pattern — no need for a separate length variable.">Loop code checks name != NULL to know when to stop, avoiding a separate length variable</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The sentinel entry is always the last thing in the array. It doesn't represent a real device.">It represents the 'none' device type</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The NULL entry isn't for error recovery. It's simply an end-of-list marker.">It's used as a fallback when an unsupported device is requested</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <!-- Q5 -->
  <div class="question" data-question="5">
    <p>Q5. <code>sdrOpen()</code> acquires a mutex before calling <code>current_handler()-&gt;open()</code>. Which mutex is it, and why does that matter?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="reader.mutex is used by lockReader()/unlockReader() to protect mag_buffer access. The open/cancel/close calls use a separate mutex.">Threads.reader.mutex — the same one used for the sample ring buffer</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="There is no global Modes.mutex. Global state uses specific purpose-built mutexes.">Modes.mutex — the global state lock</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. Modes.sdrControlMutex is acquired around open, cancel, and close. The comment in sdr.c explains why: rtlsdr_cancel_async() and rtlsdr_close() can sometimes never return, so all control operations are serialized through this mutex to prevent concurrent calls that might deadlock.">Modes.sdrControlMutex — it serializes open/cancel/close because rtlsdr library calls can hang</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="pthread_mutex_t is the type, not the name of a specific mutex in the codebase.">pthread_mutex_t — the POSIX default mutex for all threading</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

