<div class="callout" style="margin-top:1rem;">
<strong>Scenario B:</strong> A colleague suggests replacing the <code>sdr_handler</code> struct with a simple switch/case in each of <code>sdrOpen()</code>, <code>sdrRun()</code>, etc. What's the problem with that?
<br><br>
<em>Think it through, then expand:</em> <a href="#" id="toggleB" style="color:var(--cyan)">show answer</a>
<div id="ansB" hidden style="margin-top:0.75rem; color:var(--green)">
Every new SDR type requires changes in every function (<code>sdrOpen</code>, <code>sdrRun</code>, <code>sdrClose</code>, <code>sdrSetGain</code>, <code>sdrCancel</code>, <code>sdrHandleOption</code>, <code>sdrInitConfig</code>) — seven places instead of one row in the dispatch table. You also scatter device-specific logic into <code>sdr.c</code> instead of keeping it in the device's own file. The dispatch-table approach is the <em>Open/Closed Principle</em> in C: open for extension (add a row), closed for modification (don't touch existing code).
</div>
